WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.265 --> 00:00:01.879
(intense music)

00:00:01.879 --> 00:00:02.810
- [Narrator] United Airlines

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:05.510
wants to bring back supersonic air travel

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:08.520
with a new aircraft.
(bass thumping)

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.540
The airline says it plans
to buy 15 Overture jets

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.170
from the start-up aerospace
company Boom Supersonic.

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:18.330
Boom hopes to fly a scaled-down
prototype later this year,

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.240
or early in 2022,

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.740
with the full size 88 seat version

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.350
targeted to carry passengers by 2029.

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:30.150
So what does this mean for the
future of supersonic travel?

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.220
Is this acquisition a turning point,

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.380
or should the markets be more skeptical?

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.540
- United's announcement
isn't a game changer

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.350
for supersonic travel.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.140
We've had pretend orders,
almost real orders before.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.520
The proof is when United
actually spends money.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.410
- [Narrator] But Boom CEO Blake Scholl

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.190
disputed that critique.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.197
In an email to the "Journal," he wrote,

00:00:52.197 --> 00:00:55.440
"United has agreed to
purchase 15 Overture aircraft

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.260
on industry standard terms,

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.050
including nonrefundable upfront payments."

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.950
In a statement to the
"Wall Street Journal,"

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:05.410
United said, "While we cannot disclose

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:06.780
the financial details,

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.202
we can confirm that we have made a deposit

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:11.650
which signals our confidence in Boom

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.800
as well as the aircraft
they are building."

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.490
Over the past decade, several startups,

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:21.780
the most prominent of which
include Boom, Aerion, and Spike,

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.090
have been working to develop

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:26.720
the next generation of
commercial supersonic jets.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.640
But creating a new plane that can travel

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.770
faster than the speed of
sound and do it safely

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.280
is not easy or cheap.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.680
- Really, this is just
a question of funding.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.030
It's probably gonna cost
well over $20 billion

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.120
to develop a supersonic
jet, to say nothing

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.260
of the cost of actually
building it and buying it.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:45.790
- [Narrator] Last month, Aerion,

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:47.240
which is backed by Boeing,

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:50.260
folded after was unable
to raise enough money

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:53.343
to produce a planned
supersonic business jet.

00:01:53.343 --> 00:01:55.460
(upbeat music)

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.460
The British French airliner Concorde

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.710
was one of only two supersonic jets

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.720
to have operated commercially,

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.270
but it failed as a business

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.000
because of its high ticket prices

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.440
and a 2000 crash in Paris

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.693
that left 113 people dead.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:15.200
A 3 1/2 hour Concorde flight
between New York and London

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.860
cost as much as $10,000 in the year 2000.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.170
Although United has not said

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.610
how much tickets on its supersonic jets

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:24.100
would eventually cost,

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.800
they would likely be more
expensive than a typical flight.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.820
But who is the target market?

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.800
- There are really two
very distinct markets.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.230
The corporate market,
the business jet market,

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:37.820
typically has very low elasticity.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:40.258
They don't really care how
much they pay for travel,

00:02:40.258 --> 00:02:43.470
whereas a scheduled air
transport, the jetliner business,

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:46.080
the airliner business,
they are typically used

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.700
thousands of hours per year.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.962
Obviously that market is a
bit more sensitive to price.

00:02:51.962 --> 00:02:54.760
(soft intense music)

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:56.990
- [Narrator] Today, business
travelers have come to expect

00:02:56.990 --> 00:03:00.540
a certain level of luxury
on their subsonic flights.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.350
Given the smaller size
of the supersonic planes,

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:06.730
that same experience may
be hard to duplicate.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:09.620
But United isn't concerned about demand.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.620
The airline said it believes
there will be ample appetite

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.530
for supersonic trips
from business travelers

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:18.283
concentrated in United's coastal hubs.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:20.797
United told the "Journal,"

00:03:20.797 --> 00:03:23.260
"Boom is designing
Overture to be profitable

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:26.930
at fares comparable to today's
subsonic business class,

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:31.400
thanks in large part to a 75%
reduction in operating costs

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.937
relative to Concorde."

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.780
Technological hurdles
have hampered development.

00:03:37.931 --> 00:03:40.240
- There was a reason
Concorde had 100 seats.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.298
If they could have made it
bigger with the materials

00:03:42.298 --> 00:03:45.210
available at that time
and the engines available

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:46.450
at that time, they would have,

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:47.720
which would have made

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:50.620
the aircraft potentially more economic.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:53.630
So designers now have new
materials, new engines,

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:55.630
but they still face that basic physics,

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:58.170
which is why the shapes are very similar

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:00.560
between these new designs and Concorde.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.050
- [Narrator] Perhaps the
biggest technological problem

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:04.601
is reducing the Sonic boom

00:04:04.601 --> 00:04:06.920
that is made when these supersonic jets

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:08.460
break the sound barrier.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:11.240
- NASA has kind of led
the research in the US,

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:14.670
and has a test program going on right now.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.400
And what their researchers
say that the modeling shows,

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.620
instead of a boom that can break windows,

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:24.330
it will be become what
they call a soft thump

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.160
is all that you would hear on the ground.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.013
- [Narrator] For now,
experts are skeptical

00:04:29.013 --> 00:04:33.060
about the economic viability
of supersonic travel.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:36.370
- Basically, I think Boom is
feeling a bit under the gun

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:39.200
because of Aerion's
collapse the previous week,

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.370
and they wanted to have
something to take to investors

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.180
and say, hey, there's
commercial activity here.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:46.210
- [Narrator] Boom CEO Blake Scholl said

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:49.373
that "Aerion's demise came
as a surprise to the industry

00:04:49.373 --> 00:04:50.960
just a few weeks ago.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:53.157
We couldn't possibly
conclude a transaction

00:04:53.157 --> 00:04:55.153
in that period of time."

00:04:55.153 --> 00:04:58.410
- Little or no money has changed hands.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:02.270
So United has certainly
scored a big publicity coup.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:05.420
Whether it's advanced the
technology remains to be seen.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:07.490
I guess that the big help might be

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:11.030
that Boom the aircraft
maker might find it easier

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:13.250
to get the kind of finance that its rivals

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:15.531
have found it tough to find.

00:05:15.531 --> 00:05:17.948
(soft music)

